# CC1 — Neural Networks: Scikit-learn vs TensorFlow/Keras

Machine Learning project comparing two frameworks on the **same problems**:

| Notebook | Task | Dataset | Scikit-learn | TensorFlow/Keras |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `CC1_Iris.ipynb` | Classification (3 classes) | Iris | `MLPClassifier` | `Sequential` + `Dense` |
| `CC1_House_Prices.ipynb` | Regression (price prediction) | Housing | `MLPRegressor` | `Sequential` + `Dense` |

The notebooks are written in French, step by step, with commented code aimed at students.

## Project structure

```text
CC1/
├── CC1_Iris.ipynb
├── CC1_House_Prices.ipynb
├── requirements.txt
├── README.md
└── Dataset/
    ├── Housing.csv
    └── iris.csv
```

## Architectures

| Notebook | Scikit-learn | Keras |
|---|---|---|
| Iris | 4 → 10 → 10 → 3 | 4 → Dense(16, ReLU) → Dense(8, ReLU) → Dense(3, Softmax) |
| House Prices | n → 64 → 32 → 1 | n → Dense(64) → Dense(32) → Dense(16) → Dense(1) (ReLU hidden layers) |

## Results

Values obtained in one execution of the notebooks (they may vary slightly with your machine and library versions).

**Iris** — metric: accuracy (30 test samples)

| Model | Framework | Accuracy |
|---|---|---:|
| MLPClassifier | Scikit-learn | 0.9667 |
| Neural Network | TensorFlow/Keras | 0.9333 |

The gap is a single flower out of 30, so both models perform essentially the same.

**House Prices** — metrics: MAE, RMSE, R² (109 test samples, prices in original units)

| Model | Framework | MAE | RMSE | R² |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| MLPRegressor | Scikit-learn | 1,041,123 | 1,374,433 | 0.6263 |
| Neural Network | TensorFlow/Keras | 1,003,892 | 1,353,910 | 0.6373 |

Keras is slightly better on all three metrics, but the difference is small. Accuracy is deliberately **not** used here since this is a regression problem.

## Notes on the data

- The housing file is `Housing.csv` (545 houses), whose target column is `price` (not `SalePrice` as in the Kaggle *House Prices — Advanced Regression* `train.csv`). The approach is identical.
- The yes/no columns are converted to 0/1 and `furnishingstatus` is one-hot encoded, giving 13 numeric features. Missing values would be filled with the median (there are none in this file).
- Both features and target are standardized for training; predictions are converted back to real prices before computing metrics.

## Getting started

```bash
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd CC1
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate        # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
jupyter notebook
```

Run the notebooks **from the `CC1/` folder** so the path `Dataset/Housing.csv` resolves correctly. Then open `CC1_Iris.ipynb` or `CC1_House_Prices.ipynb` and use *Run All*.

## Tech stack

Python · NumPy · pandas · Matplotlib · scikit-learn · TensorFlow/Keras · Jupyter
